 during the fall, the body’s velocity is $9m/s$ and instantaneous acceleration is $6.8m/s^{2}$.\n\nFind the constant of proportionality $k$, and the differential equation that best models the velocity. Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g = 9.8m/s^{2}$, and the only forces acting on the body are weight due to gravity and drag.\n\nThe constant of proportionality is $k =$\n{{field-1}} {{field-2}}.\n\nThe differential equation that best models the velocity is\n{{field-3}}."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115780 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9aa60da6-30d7-577d-b014-6ac544bbeaed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt} = 4g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113658} {:answer/id #uuid "b76210cd-fba1-5453-a03d-fb23ba1d1798" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt} = g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113659} {:answer/id #uuid "c1d368a2-6510-5702-9525-67c8823c62c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt} = \\frac{g}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113660} {:answer/id #uuid "1b539f72-2298-5f36-9ab0-e2e32313317d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt} + \\frac{1}{4}v = g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113661} {:answer/id #uuid "34cb6213-d25d-58c7-86b8-e29e60ecf57c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt} + 4v = g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113662}] :answer-field/id #uuid "bf3d178f-4644-5b1d-84e6-f3aeb88e4c48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113657}] :question/id #uuid "af2c766e-97a5-51d1-8d6a-ea315f4a2243" :question/math-academy-id "q-334536" :question/problem "A body of mass $4kg$ falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the body's velocity, in meters per second, $t \\ge 0$ seconds after the fall began, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance acting on the body is negligible, and the only other force acting on it is the weight due to gravity. Which differential equation best models the velocity? Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115781 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "3e1f4f61-fbc8-5e07-917a-d2401c1e5caf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113663}] :question/id #uuid "57cd1e2a-f71d-5ed1-83e1-f5f79912c9f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-334539" :question/problem "A small package of mass $5kg$ is launched vertically downward with an initial velocity of $2.6m/s$. Let $v(t)$ be the package's velocity, in meters per second, $t \\ge 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance acting on the package is negligible, and the only other force acting on it is its weight due to gravity.\n\nWhat is the velocity of the package after $4$ seconds? Assume that acceleration due to gravity is $g = 9.8m/s^{2}$.\n\nThe velocity after $4$ seconds is\n{{field-1}}\n$m/s$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115782 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "0f81c3d7-8d4c-5051-b8be-04fa18c2ce85" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113664}] :question/id #uuid "04134bf9-5fbf-5298-b48c-26052ef52eb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-334550" :question/problem "A body of mass $25kg$ is released from rest and falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the body's velocity, in meters per second, $t \\ge 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the body is proportional to its velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the body’s velocity is $12m/s$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $7.4m/s^{2}$. Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g = 9.8m/s^{2}$, and the only forces acting on the body are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\nFind the equation for the velocity $v(t)$, expressing your answer in terms of $t$ **only.**\n\n$v(t) =$\n{{field-1}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115783 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "265836f7-7db8-5001-8308-e671ee3e814c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113665} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f18bb050-18cc-5928-9d8c-896a3ef3632b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f18bb050-18cc-5928-9d8c-896a3ef3632b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363762} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecd082fa-e103-5217-aeaa-809d0e229d80" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "113b60a6-133a-57f7-b5c5-f7c669afe5dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-334554" :question/problem "A supply crate of mass $18\\,\\text{kg}$ is dropped vertically downward with an initial velocity of $2.4\\,\\text{m/s}$ and instantaneous acceleration of $8.2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Let $v(t)$ be the crate's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction. The air resistance on the crate is proportional to its velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\n\nFind, rounded to one decimal place, the velocity of the supply crate after $4$ seconds.\nAssume that only gravity, $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and drag act on the crate.\n\nThe velocity of the supply crate after $4$ seconds is\n{{field-1}}$\\,\\text{m/s}.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, note that only two forces act on the crate: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw=mg=18\\cdot 9.8=176.4\\,\\text{N},\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n\n$$\nD=-kv,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Hence, the resultant force acting on the crate is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=176.4-kv.\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\ 176.4-kv & =18\\,\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{k}{18}v & =9.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that, at a particular moment, the crate’s velocity is $2.4\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $8.2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Substituting these values into the ODE, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n8.2+\\frac{k}{18}\\cdot 2.4=9.8\\,⟹\\,k=12\\,\\text{kg/s}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{2}{3}v=9.8\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${v}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{v}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{2}{3}{v}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{v}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-2t/3},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{v}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${v}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{v}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{2}{3}\\alpha =9.8\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =14.7\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${v}_{p}(t)=14.7.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nv(t)={v}_{c}(t)+{v}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-2t/3}+14.7.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the crate is released with an initial velocity of $2.4\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $v(0)=2.4,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =A{e}^{-2(0)/3}+14.7 \\\\ 2.4 & =A+14.7 \\\\ A & =-12.3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $v(t):$\n\n$$\nv(t)=-12.3{e}^{-2t/3}+14.7\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of the supply crate after $t=4$ seconds is\n\n$$\nv(4)=-12.3{e}^{-2(4)/3}+14.7=13.845…≈13.8\\,\\text{m/s},\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115784 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "20f15981-13a3-5e21-9eea-ac9183e6f501" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113666}] :question/id #uuid "e1a75ba0-fe9b-504a-a44f-77251d64934c" :question/math-academy-id "q-334572" :question/problem "A body of mass $20kg$ falls vertically downward. Let $v$ be its velocity, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the body is proportional to its velocity with a constant of proportionality $k = 5kg/s$ and acts opposite to the direction of motion.\n\nWhat is the terminal velocity $v_{term}$ of the body? Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g = 9.8m/s^{2}$, and the only forces acting on the body are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\n$v_{term} =$\n{{field-1}}\n$m/s$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115785 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "45e65d4c-3298-5398-82d7-b45cfbd26c0f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113667} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df8663d4-6cd9-51c3-8f9c-05a3eec71857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df8663d4-6cd9-51c3-8f9c-05a3eec71857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363764} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1d2a0e2-c9a1-5f21-95bd-334eccb632ba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cfb581b3-3b37-5c68-b506-d7706bce3e3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-334575" :question/problem "A ball of mass $16\\,\\text{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v$ be its velocity, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the ball is proportional to its velocity and acts in the opposite direction to the motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the ball’s velocity is $16\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $4.9\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nWhat is the terminal velocity ${v}_{\\text{term}}$ of the body? Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the body are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\n${v}_{\\text{term}}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/s}$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Terminal velocity is the constant speed a falling object approaches as the upward resistive force (drag) grows to exactly balance the downward gravitational force.\nThat is, at terminal velocity (a constant), acceleration is zero, so by $F=ma,$ the resultant force is zero.\n\nFirst, note that only two forces act on the ball: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw=mg=16\\cdot 9.8=156.8\\,\\text{N},\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n\n$$\nD=-kv,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Hence, the resultant force acting on the ball is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=156.8-kv.\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\ 156.8-kv & =16a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that, at a particular moment, the ball’s velocity is $16\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $4.9\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}156.8-16k & =16\\cdot 4.9 \\\\ 156.8-16k & =78.4 \\\\ -16k & =-78.4 \\\\ k & =4.9\\,\\text{kg/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the resultant force acting on the ball is\n\n$$\nF=156.8-4.9v.\n$$\n\nWhen the ball reaches terminal velocity ${v}_{\\text{term}},$ the resultant force is zero:\n\n$$\n156.8-4.9{v}_{\\text{term}}=0\\,⟹\\,{v}_{\\text{term}}=\\frac{156.8}{4.9}=32\n$$\n\nTherefore, the terminal velocity of the ball is ${v}_{\\text{term}}=32\\,\\text{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115786 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "6df614d1-97ad-5df6-9fe1-d9f076fa7b0a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113668}] :question/id #uuid "64d0d3c7-e6a8-546d-9659-11ed40890d84" :question/math-academy-id "q-334770" :question/problem "A metal capsule of mass $6kg$ is released and falls vertically downward with an initial velocity of $1.2m/s$. Let $v(t)$ be the capsule's velocity, in meters per second, $t \\ge 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction. The air resistance acting on the capsule is negligible, and the only other force acting on it is its weight due to gravity.\n\nFind the equation for $v(t)$. Express your answer in terms of time $t$ and acceleration due to gravity $g$.\n\n$v(t) =$\n{{field-1}}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115789 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "d6fbb24c-bc55-59f9-bde8-a6c719ed0e29" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113681}] :question/id #uuid "f7832100-7147-5d72-90f5-3e6ed0e5d0c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-335190" :question/problem "A weather sensor of mass $30kg$ is launched downward with an initial velocity of $5m/s$ and continues to fall vertically. Let $v(t)$ be the sensor's velocity, in meters per second, $t \\ge 0$ seconds after launch, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the sensor is proportional to its velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the sensor’s velocity is $8m/s$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $8.2m/s^{2}$. Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g = 9.8m/s^{2}$, and the only forces acting on the sensor are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\nFind the equation for the velocity $v(t)$, expressing your answer in terms of $t$ **only.**\n\n$v(t) =$\n{{field-1}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124256 :question/id #uuid "a5753b43-b288-5cca-a26b-b060917182ab" :question/math-academy-id "e-3330" :question/problem "Find  $$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,2)}} \\dfrac{xy^3}{x+y}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)= \\dfrac{xy^3}{x+y}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(-1,2)$ does not cause the denominator to become zero, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-1,2)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,2)}\\frac{x{y}^{3}}{x+y} & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot {2}^{3}}{(-1)+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8}{1} \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124257 :question/id #uuid "301263ef-3b68-54ac-87aa-5964484f5a14" :question/math-academy-id "e-3331" :question/problem "Find  $$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}} \\dfrac{x^2y+xy^2}{x^2-y^2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\dfrac 0 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}} \\dfrac{x^2y+xy^2}{x^2-y^2} = \\dfrac{(-1)^2(1) + (-1)(1)^2}{(-1)^2 - (1)^2} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{xy(x+y)}{(x+y)(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{xy}{(x-y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{xy}{(x-y)} & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 1}{-1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\dfrac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124261 :question/id #uuid "56539c63-dd81-57c9-b7d2-18c935953203" :question/math-academy-id "e-3366" :question/problem "Find  $$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Direct substitution gives an indeterminate form, but the function cannot be simplified by canceling a common factor in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2} = \\dfrac{0 \\cdot (0 + 2 \\cdot 0)}{0^2} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{x(x+2y)}{{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(x+2x)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-x}\\frac{x(x+2y)}{{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(x+2(-x))}{(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on these two paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2} = \\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124367 :question/id #uuid "ae167d95-9cfd-5f31-91b1-484becd62677" :question/math-academy-id "e-6239" :question/problem "Find  $$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}} \\dfrac{x^2-4y}{x-2\\sqrt{y}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}} \\dfrac{2^2-4 \\cdot 1}{2-2\\sqrt{1}} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4y}{x-2\\sqrt{y}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{({x}^{2}-4y)(x+2\\sqrt{y})}{(x-2\\sqrt{y})(x+2\\sqrt{y})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{({x}^{2}-4y)(x+2\\sqrt{y})}{{x}^{2}-(2\\sqrt{y})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{({x}^{2}-4y)(x+2\\sqrt{y})}{{x}^{2}-4y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}(x+2\\sqrt{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}(x+2\\sqrt{y}) & =2+2\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =2+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124937 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c03dc64-0f3b-5e8f-a8ea-480cf8c23bda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122223} {:answer/id #uuid "c892c477-c790-5a52-9139-e23e36c04a8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122224} {:answer/id #uuid "2ac772a4-2c3f-5016-95a7-5056a887f722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122225} {:answer/id #uuid "577e0ce2-bfb6-5fd9-aa5b-620334d6829f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122226} {:answer/id #uuid "6f6d19bd-365e-5681-9a90-a675402ec3f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c03dc64-0f3b-5e8f-a8ea-480cf8c23bda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122223} :answer-field/id #uuid "71ff1c87-4a2b-5ff3-8f60-90ba048bfa23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1f613246-2fd9-5309-ad22-34ad0114450f" :question/math-academy-id "q-35952" :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(1,1)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(1,1)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=\\frac{{1}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}}{{1}^{3}+{1}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124938 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2668e40a-2488-5d2e-bb1f-0121fc724561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448572} {:answer/id #uuid "9a2c69fe-bead-5476-89ac-9bfc46f40175" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2668e40a-2488-5d2e-bb1f-0121fc724561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448572} :answer-field/id #uuid "034e2028-2f8f-51e2-9def-352dd1c887b9" :answer-field/key "The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y=x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449326} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2baa656c-df91-580b-a9c3-4500a888e82b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448574} {:answer/id #uuid "3cc92910-8986-56a6-8799-16bcee2e1e70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cc92910-8986-56a6-8799-16bcee2e1e70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448575} :answer-field/id #uuid "d69e547b-615d-50fb-b328-e31355652e5b" :answer-field/key "The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y=-x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449327} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7b60fef-2a9f-5f32-8282-17b9017027d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448576} {:answer/id #uuid "8cbe814b-8f7e-5508-a800-8e2be0eccd01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7b60fef-2a9f-5f32-8282-17b9017027d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448576} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e31b0e0-9526-5208-b591-b3210a53bee0" :answer-field/key "The limit exists and is equal to $1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6a81953f-1927-53ea-b174-321fab218b53" :question/math-academy-id "q-35953" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{xy-x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{xy-x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy}=\\frac{0\\cdot 0-0\\cdot {0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+2\\cdot 0\\cdot 0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y=x$” is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{xy-x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(x)-x(x)^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2x(x)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}}{3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-x}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-0}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y=-x$” is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-x}\\frac{xy-x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(-x)-x(-x)^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2x(-x)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}}{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(1+x) \\\\  & =1+0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit exists and is equal to $1$” is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{xy-x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124953 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc366fb7-3b96-5cce-8c18-91533c332205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379792} {:answer/id #uuid "11d83d6b-c9ac-505b-a024-a94f01ef8b63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379793} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1c860c-a8be-5c8e-a6b1-b6df7568e65b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379794} {:answer/id #uuid "d13b9873-b619-5955-9c0b-cf1d23785560" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379795} {:answer/id #uuid "0437f9db-af07-5f6d-8f9b-9e9b0ad83cf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0437f9db-af07-5f6d-8f9b-9e9b0ad83cf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379796} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8afbaeb-57f5-529f-a20d-f5f369208770" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c919dc70-fb51-55ac-9d45-4a7c363f49b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-36135" :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to (-2,3)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy}=$" :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(-2,3)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-2,3)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-2,3)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy} & =\\frac{2\\cdot (-2)^{2}+(-2)\\cdot {3}^{2}}{(-2)^{2}+2\\cdot (-2)\\cdot (3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-18}{4-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10}{-8} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125220 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5e4bf55-9a10-5bcf-b1ba-805a65a3bf3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123921} {:answer/id #uuid "3eff33c7-fc2b-5d42-a965-3adddff1c0e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123922} {:answer/id #uuid "4d168aed-dd23-5ed7-b72b-7e03278d8a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123923} {:answer/id #uuid "5ef815d8-371a-5069-b3e0-9b266d62c779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123924} {:answer/id #uuid "6f85d996-bdc5-5a2c-ae0a-5f2abafa3993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5e4bf55-9a10-5bcf-b1ba-805a65a3bf3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123921} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf75eb81-4b37-5ee3-b292-043ac65176b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d068be46-087a-5639-aff2-b79d4b9eda8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-74898" :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{x\\to (-1,2,2)}\\frac{{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{z}^{3}-{y}^{2}z}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1,2,2)}\\frac{{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{z}^{3}-{y}^{2}z}=\\frac{{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}}{{2}^{3}-{2}^{2}\\cdot 2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,2,2)}\\frac{{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{z}^{3}-{y}^{2}z} & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,2,2)}\\frac{{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{z({z}^{2}-{y}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,2,2)}\\frac{1}{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,2,2)}\\frac{1}{z} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125221 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1ef4124-221a-5a91-b97a-ab217edc9f27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380030} {:answer/id #uuid "541c1996-da8d-5186-bf33-6697813ff100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380031} {:answer/id #uuid "bda409eb-ec4f-5401-9dcf-b40ae4c50f43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380032} {:answer/id #uuid "86aeb84b-e9b3-5051-aaa0-7952946334ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380033} {:answer/id #uuid "aea31071-42df-54b5-9274-ca95aa9906f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aea31071-42df-54b5-9274-ca95aa9906f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380034} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d23ad6c-16ea-5ede-a1f2-1c4a033d4add" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b7553878-547e-5260-bbb8-8346cb43f141" :question/math-academy-id "q-74901" :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to (0,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{1+xy}-1}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{1+xy}-1}=\\frac{{0}^{2}-0}{\\sqrt{1+0\\cdot 1}-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{1+xy}-1} & =\\lim_{x\\to (0,1)}\\frac{({x}^{2}-x)(\\sqrt{1+xy}+1)}{(\\sqrt{1+xy}-1)(\\sqrt{1+xy}+1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (0,1)}\\frac{x(x-1)(\\sqrt{1+xy}+1)}{(\\sqrt{1+xy})^{2}-{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (0,1)}\\frac{x(x-1)(\\sqrt{1+xy}+1)}{xy} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (0,1)}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{1+xy}+1)}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,1)}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{1+xy}+1)}{y} & =\\frac{(0-1)(\\sqrt{1+0\\cdot 1}+1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{1} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125222 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9fccf3d-3b2a-5b33-bd12-6c4954e857bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- ∞" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123933} {:answer/id #uuid "ad068003-5c0c-53ee-907b-d63969de41a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123934} {:answer/id #uuid "fd942472-438c-5a6a-9d26-d8d5ab38caf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∞" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123935} {:answer/id #uuid "7277b478-9b7f-5594-8d6c-7379c5d2b641" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "DNE" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123936} {:answer/id #uuid "7b912c6a-1628-5bd1-b92d-f0ef2fa190fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123937}] :answer-field/id #uuid "cf1e71ce-8434-5ef5-9122-75d8bacfe896" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123932} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f08974e8-e936-5c85-b6a9-d720913a44f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380036} {:answer/id #uuid "073ecdcc-0e82-5540-a063-14787b43ccc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380037} {:answer/id #uuid "74037f58-94e0-5f4b-b642-529c4760d8d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380038} {:answer/id #uuid "eb86860f-a9f9-52b5-b8ca-e8fac436332c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380039} {:answer/id #uuid "2b214f52-5fef-5a7f-b0cb-9e2a51b1e5f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b214f52-5fef-5a7f-b0cb-9e2a51b1e5f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380040} :answer-field/id #uuid "0437ed33-2c17-55e7-bef6-493c7f7ed724" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "38bfaf73-a17c-57af-a4a5-e1f3edf77e4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-74903" :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2{y}^{2}x+y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2{y}^{2}x+y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot 0+0\\cdot {0}^{2}}{{0}^{3}+0\\cdot {0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{2{y}^{2}x+y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-x}\\frac{2{y}^{2}x+y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{3}-{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2{y}^{2}x+y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125223 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b854f763-ed80-5765-977e-d777be9389dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123939} {:answer/id #uuid "b9820c9d-fbc4-5ec7-8e94-b92be2bcca27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123940} {:answer/id #uuid "bb91eaa9-b9b0-507a-a5d1-1276815c0414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123941} {:answer/id #uuid "bc8208af-32ab-5380-8ca8-41fb48e098cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123942} {:answer/id #uuid "7f9a7dee-ade9-57bb-b47b-7818602e9219" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f9a7dee-ade9-57bb-b47b-7818602e9219" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123943} :answer-field/id #uuid "406cbd00-ec1e-58cf-b40f-a0b22e94c3e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6e3fea14-2815-5683-ac38-ab51981e0e10" :question/math-academy-id "q-74924" :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{x\\to (2,2,-1)}\\frac{xyz-z{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (2,2,-1)}\\frac{xyz-z{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot {2}^{2}}{{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,2,-1)}\\frac{xyz-z{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,2,-1)}\\frac{yz(x-y)}{-(x-y)(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,2,-1)}\\frac{yz}{-x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,2,-1)}\\frac{yz}{-x-y} & =\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)}{-2-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125224 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "907c8b24-14ed-559c-8b1b-a819efed8b6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123945} {:answer/id #uuid "96fbb520-5f93-5504-87e0-aa25dea97fc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123946} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5cbec9-9bb3-5392-a3a0-ea0d2b0b9eee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123947} {:answer/id #uuid "70fc3b47-ffbe-56d3-b7b9-1921938dd685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123948} {:answer/id #uuid "73d82dab-632d-5399-86f9-c24e1e313515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123949}] :answer-field/id #uuid "1932e0e7-8c06-5d02-816c-9de59dff87d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123944} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbd3afd7-5a69-5bc2-8161-fee77f4632dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377703} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b0c048-b115-51b0-b2dc-9897de240e0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377704} {:answer/id #uuid "23b23618-823a-5db2-9ef3-35b32a0c9e44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377705} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ed3530-f0fa-5d8c-bdb6-2cf6002bca16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377706} {:answer/id #uuid "bcab442c-986f-5282-b630-dfaf3c36ca2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23b23618-823a-5db2-9ef3-35b32a0c9e44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377705} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc72be4b-ba1c-5d2a-b23f-ef19af81df35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dfaf27b0-dc8b-5691-99ed-4010ac8fdea0" :question/math-academy-id "q-74954" :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (4,4)}\\frac{x-y}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (4,4)}\\frac{x-y}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}}=\\frac{4-4}{\\sqrt{4}-\\sqrt{4}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (4,4)}\\frac{x-y}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (4,4)}\\frac{(x-y)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y})(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (4,4)}\\frac{(x-y)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{(\\sqrt{x})^{2}-(\\sqrt{y})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (4,4)}\\frac{(x-y)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{x-y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (4,4)}(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (4,4)}(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}) & =\\sqrt{4}+\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147210 :question/id #uuid "39259a31-5d67-5482-b1bb-4fa719ce4f95" :question/math-academy-id "e-20894" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{y}{ x^2 } \\textrm{d}x + Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $Q(x,y)?$\n\n- $\\dfrac{1}{x}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{y}{x}$\n\n- $e^{yx}$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $P(x,y) = -\\dfrac{y}{x^2}.$\n\n First, let's differentiate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} =\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(-\\dfrac{y}{x^2}\\right)=-\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}$ to each of the given options and determine whether the results match up with $\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}$ above:\n\n- If $Q(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{x},$ then we get an exact equation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\dfrac 1 x\\right) = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2} = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n- If $Q(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x},$ then we do *not* get an exact equation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\dfrac y x\\right) = -\\dfrac{y}{x^2} \\neq \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}\n$$\n\n- If $Q(x,y) = e^{xy},$ then we do *not* get an exact equation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(e^{xy}\\right) = ye^{xy} \\neq \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147211 :question/id #uuid "ca703f4f-5363-5fc1-8372-136e7c87156d" :question/math-academy-id "e-20895" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the exact differential equation\n\n$$\n(y^2-2x)\\,\\textrm{d}x + (2xy +1)\\textrm{d}y = 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the differential equation is of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y) = y^2-2x$ and $Q(x,y) = 2xy+1.$\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} = P(x,y),\\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} = Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int {y}^{2}-2x\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =x{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y$.  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int 2xy+1\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =x{y}^{2}+y+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x) = -x^2, \\qquad f(y) = y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y) = c$ is\n\n$$\nxy^2-x^2 + y = c.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147213 :question/id #uuid "36440ae0-1cee-52c0-b24a-7871e172a341" :question/math-academy-id "e-20933" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations is exact?  \n\n- $(2xy)\\,\\textrm{d}x + (x^{2}+4)\\,\\textrm{d}y = 0$\n\n- $(y^{2}+2x)\\,\\textrm{d}x + (xy+1)\\,\\textrm{d}y = 0$\n\n- $(7y - x)\\,\\textrm{d}y - (y - 3x)\\,\\textrm{d}x = 0$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation:\n\n- The first equation is\n\n$$\n(2xy)\\;\\textrm{d}x + (x^{2}+4)\\;\\textrm{d}y = 0.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = 2xy, \\qquad Q(x,y) = x^{2}+4.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy) \\\\  & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+4) \\\\  & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} {=} \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}. \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n \nThus, equation I is exact.\n\n- The second equation is\n\n$$\n(y^{2}+2x)\\;\\textrm{d}x + (xy+1)\\;\\textrm{d}y = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = y^{2}+2x, \\qquad Q(x,y) = xy+1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+2x) \\\\  & =2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+1) \\\\  & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} {\\neq} \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}. \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}\n$$\n\nThus, equation II is not exact.\n\n- The third equation is\n\n$$\n(7y - x)\\;\\textrm{d}y - (y - 3x)\\;\\textrm{d}x = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = -(y - 3x) = 3x - y, \\qquad Q(x,y) = 7y - x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x-y) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(7y-x) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} {=} \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}. \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\nThus, equation III is exact.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147214 :question/id #uuid "c1b768cb-8709-55f0-878b-d9608ac1c478" :question/math-academy-id "e-20934" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the differential equation\n\n$$\ny' = \\frac{11+ye^{xy}}{13y - xe^{xy}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nStarting from \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{11+y{e}^{xy}}{13y-x{e}^{xy}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by $(13y-xe^{xy})\\;\\textrm{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(13y-xe^{xy})\\;\\textrm{d}y = (11+ye^{xy})\\;\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we then rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(11+ye^{xy})\\;\\textrm{d}x + (xe^{xy}-13y)\\;\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y) =11+ye^{xy}, \\qquad Q(x,y) = xe^{xy}-13y.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(11+y{e}^{xy}) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{xy}-13y) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} {=} \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}. \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n \nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} = P(x,y),\\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} = Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (11+y{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =11x+{e}^{xy}+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (x{e}^{xy}-13y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}-\\frac{13}{2}{y}^{2}+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x) = 11x, \\quad  f(y) = - \\dfrac{13}{2}y^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y) = c$ is\n\n$$\ne^{xy} - \\dfrac{13}{2}y^2 + 11x = c.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147765 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88e16604-bcf6-5416-82f9-cb2b6ef067ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{xy} - x^{2} + 2x = c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144293} {:answer/id #uuid "a049cd6a-5fb9-533f-92eb-8a7d89ab003a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{xy} - x + 2y = c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144294} {:answer/id #uuid "d9200766-a989-5c78-bb9c-348897f8283e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{xy} - 2x + 2y = c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144295} {:answer/id #uuid "fed8c1cc-c347-5cf2-ad96-04159c577938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{xy} - x^{2} + 2y = c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144296} {:answer/id #uuid "756a9eb6-571e-505a-a8fd-46e6150faaaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{xy} - y^{2} + 2x = c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144297}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5c863c3d-2f1b-526a-9dec-094c79a82317" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144292}] :question/id #uuid "447380d3-5759-50fd-af9b-8624adbd7fae" :question/math-academy-id "q-317664" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the differential equation\n$y^{′} = \\frac{2 + ye^{xy}}{2y - xe^{xy}}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147766 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d34fec9b-5e0e-5f62-afea-a768b0a5f9e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{∂P}{∂x} = \\frac{∂Q}{∂y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144299} {:answer/id #uuid "0326426c-4d00-5271-ab47-655787348cf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{∂P}{∂y} = \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144300} {:answer/id #uuid "087d04ca-6f17-51a7-a110-77e0964c516c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{∂P}{∂x} =-\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144301} {:answer/id #uuid "393677f3-bfec-5fc5-9297-39748dc45b80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{∂^{2}P}{∂y^{2}} = \\frac{∂^{2}Q}{∂x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144302}] :answer-field/id #uuid "309ea4c1-501b-517a-bfbf-0552de27cd1b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144298}] :question/id #uuid "bf51fff6-cc9c-57f2-8794-40aa87c275a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-317821" :question/problem "Determine whether the following differential equation is exact:\n$(2 + ye^{xy})dx + (xe^{xy} - 2y)dy = 0$A differential equation of the form\n\n$P(x, y)dx + 